      body: 'Here is a second made-up story. A woman named Ingrid in a fishing town in Norway kept a ledger in a kitchen drawer. Her father had been a hard man who drank and lost the family boat, and her grandfather before him had done much the same. In the ledger she had written, year by year, every failure of her own that she could trace to them, as if she were adding up a debt she had been born owing. When her daughter married, Ingrid meant to hand the book on, so the girl would know what she carried. An old neighbor, Solveig, saw it on the table and asked to read one page. She read it and closed it. She said only that the boat was truly gone, and the years were truly hard, and that neither was the girl’s to pay for. Then she opened her own worn Book to the prophet and laid it on top of the ledger. Ingrid read it standing. That night she burned the ledger in the stove and kept the Book on the table. "The soul that sinneth, it shall die. The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father, neither shall the father bear the iniquity of the son: the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the wickedness of the wicked shall be upon him." (Ezekiel 18:20)',
